### Linux and open-source movement

As a software developer, 


Since software developers hold "the means of (digital) production" it makes
sense that open-source has self-organised to cater to our needs initially,
 but I wouldn't be surprised if the next decade sees other means of
digital production, such as creative tools, 3D modelling, and office suites.
Tools such as Calligra, Inkscape and Krita are already of significant quality.

An odd but natural pushback to this attack on digital sovereignty over our
careers, we see in India is piracy. In the 2000s there was a small economy of
"computer repair guys" who'd come over with a pirated windows/office disc and
set up your computer. Adobe Photoshop, CorelDraw and AutoCad are still commonly
pirated and used in shops that make digital printouts of passport photographs,
designing of business cards, and even government land surveys. Buying this
software legally would be prohibitively expensive and a completely non-starter.
Ironically, I feel that the pirates have the moral high-ground here, though I'm
not sure how much thought was put into it.


